      <div class="kpi-value">1940s</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Most Diverse Decade</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="overview">
    <h2 class="section-title">Dataset Overview & Historical Context</h2>
    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score honors the finest cinematic musical achievements across nearly a century of filmmaking. This analysis covers 93 consecutive ceremonies from the 6th Academy Awards in 1934 through the 98th ceremony in March 2026.</p>
    <p>The dataset reveals a striking tension in Oscar history: <strong>dominance versus diversity</strong>. While a handful of composers have shaped the award's legacy through repeated wins, the vast majority of winners (79 unique composers across 93 ceremonies) represent a remarkably varied landscape of musical talent.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Data Note:</strong> Oppenheimer appears as winner for both 2023 and 2024 in the source data, both times with Ludwig Göransson credited. This appears to reflect the ceremony years rather than film release years.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="top-composers">
    <h2 class="section-title">Most Awarded Composers</h2>
    <p>A three-way tie for the most wins defines the summit of Oscar scoring history. John Barry, John Williams, and Ludwig Göransson each claimed four Oscars, though across different eras with distinctly different win patterns.</p>

    <div class="tier-list">
      <div class="composer-card tier-top">
        <div class="composer-rank">Tied for 1st Place</div>
        <div class="composer-name">John Barry</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">4 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1966, 1968, 1985, 1990</div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-card tier-top">
        <div class="composer-rank">Tied for 1st Place</div>
        <div class="composer-name">John Williams</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">4 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1975, 1977, 1982, 1993</div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-card tier-top">
        <div class="composer-rank">Tied for 1st Place</div>
        <div class="composer-name">Ludwig Göransson</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">4 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">2018, 2023, 2024, 2026</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container" style="margin-top: 32px;">
      <canvas id="topComposersChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Second Tier: Three-Win Composers</h3>
    <div class="tier-list">
      <div class="composer-card">
        <div class="composer-rank">3 Wins</div>
        <div class="composer-name">Max Steiner</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">3 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1935, 1943, 1945</div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-card">
        <div class="composer-rank">3 Wins</div>
        <div class="composer-name">Dimitri Tiomkin</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">3 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1952, 1954, 1958</div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-card">
        <div class="composer-rank">3 Wins</div>
        <div class="composer-name">Maurice Jarre</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">3 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1962, 1965, 1984</div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-card">
        <div class="composer-rank">3 Wins</div>
        <div class="composer-name">Alan Menken</div>
        <div class="composer-wins">3 <span>wins</span></div>
        <div class="composer-years">1989, 1991, 1992</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="temporal-patterns">
    <h2 class="section-title">Temporal Patterns & Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
    <p>Win distribution across decades reveals alternating cycles of concentration and dispersion. The 1940s and 1980s stand out as the most diverse, each producing 12 unique winners, while the 1950s and 2020s show the tightest clustering.</p>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-container">
        <h3>Unique Winners per Decade</h3>
        <canvas id="decadeUniqueChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-container">
        <h3>Total Wins per Decade</h3>
        <canvas id="decadeWinsChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <h3>Decade-by-Decade Winner Distribution</h3>
      <canvas id="decadeStackedChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="era-analysis">
    <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p>Breaking the nine decades into four distinct eras reveals shifting tides in composer dominance, film genres, and musical style.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card">
        <h4>Golden Age (1934–1959)</h4>
        <div class="era-stats">
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">26</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Wins</div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">25</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Unique Composers</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">An era of extraordinary diversity. Nearly every year brought a new winner, with Max Steiner and Dimitri Tiomkin the most frequent visitors to the podium. Orchestral romanticism defined the era—sweeping scores for epics, dramas, and adventure films.</p>
        <div class="era-top-composers">
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">Max Steiner</span><span class="era-composer-wins">3 wins</span></div>
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">Dimitri Tiomkin</span><span class="era-composer-wins">3 wins</span></div>
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">Alfred Newman</span><span class="era-composer-wins">2 wins</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card">
        <h4>Silver Age (1960–1979)</h4>
        <div class="era-stats">
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">20</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Wins</div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">18</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Unique Composers</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">The emergence of distinctive auteur voices. John Barry's elegant sophistication and Maurice Jarre's epic sensibilities rose alongside a new generation. The Godfather's Nino Rota introduced a more atmospheric, Mediterranean sensibility.</p>
        <div class="era-top-composers">
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">John Barry</span><span class="era-composer-wins">2 wins</span></div>
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">Maurice Jarre</span><span class="era-composer-wins">2 wins</span></div>
          <div class="era-composer"><span class="era-composer-name">Nino Rota</span><span class="era-composer-wins">2 wins</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card">
        <h4>Modern (1980–1999)</h4>
        <div class="era-stats">
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">20</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Wins</div>
          </div>
          <div class="era-stat">
            <div class="era-stat-value">18</div>
            <div class="era-stat-label">Unique Composers</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">The Disney renaissance reshaped the landscape. Alan Menken's three consecutive wins (1989–1992) marked the first streak of its kind since the Golden Age. John Williams returned to form with iconic synthesizer-orchestra hybrids. International voices like Ennio Moroder gained recognition.</p>
        <div class="era-top-composers">
